// The hole rust-lang/rust 164250 names: a struct's bound `T : 'static` is
// checked when `Q(None)` is built and assumed by its destructor; the covariant
// assignment `q.0 = Some(&l)` then re-instantiates `Q` at the local's lifetime,
// and the destructor stores a view of `l` where only a `'static` value may go.
// Here the region a slot keeps is part of the slot's type, a built value's type
// never changes under it, and a destructor is a region's end action that reaches
// only what the region keeps: the write is refused before anything runs (#1492).
//
// What the hole is made of: evidence in two currencies. A bound written on a
// type's definition is a side condition outside the value, re-proved at every
// explicit use (a call, a coercion) but inherited as an assumption by the one
// consumer with no use site, the destructor — an explicit `Drop` must repeat
// the type's bounds exactly and so assumes them, and the generated drop glue has
// none — while subtyping by variance changes the type beneath it and nothing
// re-establishes the bound. The same shape is the ergonomic rule "bounds on the
// impl, never on the struct": a bound on the definition is re-stated on every
// implementation and constrains every use. Here a constraint is a field of the
// value's record, carried and projected, never implied from a definition and
// never assumed by an implementation; the region is that field for a slot.

/// The outer region keeps a slot; the inner scope holds `l` and lends a view of
/// it; keeping that view in the outer slot is refused at the write — the view
/// would outlive the access that lent it.
@[ refuses("ViewEscaped") ]
def bypassed() -> Integer {
  handle outer : Proc {
    run q : (() => Integer) at outer <- pure thunk { pure 0 };
    run x : Integer <- handle inner : Proc {
      hold l = 42;
      run q2 : (() => Integer) at outer <- access shared l as w { pure thunk { pure w } };
      force q2
    } with {
      pure(y) => pure y,
      oper exit(code, k) => pure 0
    };
    force q
  } with {
    pure(y) => pure y,
    oper exit(code, k) => pure 0
  }
}
